Title: "Infix Specification Command Headers"
Id: "78cabafc-70dc-4040-994f-e4d635466075"

Text: "An infix specification command header uses the `\:...:/` form.

```mlg-fragment
[A \:subset:/ B]
Defines: A
when: B is \set
extends: A is \set
Documented:
. written: \"A? \subseteq B?\"
Id: \"23e4567e-e89b-42d3-a456-426614174000\"
```

The header uses a single backslash: `\:` opens the form and `:/` closes it. Both
operands are required.

These forms are special for `Defines:`, and only `Defines:` may use them. The
left-hand side is the item being defined, so it does not require a `when:`
entry for itself. Other header parameters still need `when:` specifications.

At use sites the specification form `A \:subset:/ B` introduces symbols and is
only allowed in binding positions (`exists:`, `given:`, `forAll:`, `let:`,
`where:`, `when:`, `suchThat:`). In statement positions the predicate form
`A \:subset?:/ B` (note `?:/`) must be used instead.

A specification operator can be **refined** with the same parenthesized
refinement syntax as an ordinary command, on a `Refines:` group. The base
operator is inferred from the heading:

```mlg-fragment
[A \:(nonempty)::subset:/ B]
Refines: A
when: B is \set
Documented:
. adjective: \"nonempty\"
. written: \"A? \subset_{+} B?\"
```

An explicit refined spec-infix definition is not required when the refinement is
inherited through the base operator's `extends:` type. If `\:subset:/` extends
its left operand to `\set` and `\(nonempty)::set` is defined, then
`X' \:(nonempty)::subset:/ X` resolves implicitly, establishing both
`X' \:subset:/ X` and `X' is \(nonempty)::set`."
Id: "a28bef7f-e165-42b7-b187-0c9f446e3a93"
